{
  "id": "dl-boolean",
  "title": "Boolean Algebra",
  "description": "Use Boolean identities and De Morgan's laws to simplify expressions and build logic from NAND or NOR gates.",
  "reference": "Hambley §7.3; H&H §8.12",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "Basic identities",
      "body": "Boolean variables are only 0 or 1, so some rules differ from ordinary algebra: $A + A = A$ and $A + 1 = 1$. Every identity can be proved with a truth table by checking all input combinations.",
      "formula": "\\begin{array}{ll} A\\cdot 1 = A & A + 0 = A \\\\ A\\cdot 0 = 0 & A + 1 = 1 \\\\ AA = A & A + A = A \\\\ A\\overline{A} = 0 & A + \\overline{A} = 1 \\\\ \\overline{\\overline{A}} = A & \\end{array}"
    },
    {
      "title": "Laws for rearranging",
      "body": "AND and OR are commutative and associative, and AND distributes over OR: $A(B+C) = AB + AC$. Unlike ordinary algebra, OR also distributes over AND: $A + BC = (A+B)(A+C)$. The absorption rules are the workhorses of simplification.",
      "formula": "A + AB = A, \\qquad A(A+B) = A, \\qquad A + \\overline{A}B = A + B"
    },
    {
      "title": "De Morgan's laws",
      "body": "The complement of a product is the sum of the complements, and vice versa. General recipe: complement every variable, swap AND ↔ OR, and complement the whole expression. Watch the bars: $\\overline{AB}$ (one long bar) is not $\\overline{A}\\,\\overline{B}$ (two short bars).",
      "formula": "\\overline{AB} = \\overline{A} + \\overline{B}, \\qquad \\overline{A+B} = \\overline{A}\\,\\overline{B}"
    },
    {
      "title": "Simplifying expressions",
      "body": "Fewer terms and literals mean fewer gates. Typical moves: factor out common variables, use $X + \\overline{X} = 1$, then absorption.\nExample: $\\overline{A}BC + ABC + AB\\overline{C} = BC(\\overline{A}+A) + AB\\overline{C} = BC + AB\\overline{C} = B(C + A\\overline{C}) = B(C + A)$.\nConsensus rule: in $AB + \\overline{A}C + BC$ the term $BC$ is redundant.",
      "formula": "AB + \\overline{A}C + BC = AB + \\overline{A}C"
    },
    {
      "title": "NAND and NOR are universal",
      "body": "Any function can be built from NAND gates alone (or NOR gates alone). NOT: tie the inputs together, $\\overline{AA} = \\overline{A}$. AND: a NAND followed by a NAND-inverter. OR: invert both inputs, then NAND, since $\\overline{\\overline{A}\\,\\overline{B}} = A + B$ (circuit below, 3 gates). With NOR gates the roles of AND and OR swap.",
      "formula": "A + B = \\overline{\\overline{A}\\cdot\\overline{B}}",
      "circuit": {
